select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p3-b10-027","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What power factor must a 3-phase electricity supply above 100 A maintain under ECBC?","back":"Between 0.95 lagging and unity, at the point of connection.","explain":"Supplies above 1,000 kVA also need permanent metering of demand (kVA), energy (kWh) and total power factor. Supplies between 65 and 1,000 kVA need demand (kW), energy and power factor (or kVARh) metering.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p320","order":3492},
{"id":"p3-b10-028","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"Name four energy conservation measures for building water pumping systems.","back":"High-efficiency pumps; operating pumps in parallel; automatic operation with low-level/high-level flow control; and variable frequency drives on chilled water and cooling water pumps.","explain":"Building pumps are usually centrifugal at about 60% efficiency, so efficiency upgrades and drives are the main levers. Pumps serve chilled water, cooling water, domestic water, hot water and sewage.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p321","order":3493},
{"id":"p3-b10-029","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four main components of a UPS and state what the inverter does.","back":"Converter (AC to DC), battery, inverter (DC to AC), and monitor and control hardware and software. The inverter converts DC to AC.","explain":"The charger and converter bring utility AC to DC for the battery. The inverter then rebuilds AC for the load when the battery supplies power.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p321","order":3494},
{"id":"p3-b10-030","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two UPS architectures, and which one is more efficient?","back":"Off-line (line-interactive) and on-line (double-conversion). The off-line type is more efficient because the load connects directly to the AC input.","explain":"A line-interactive UPS uses an auto-transformer AVR to correct small voltage deviations. On an unstable grid, its frequent switches to battery shorten battery life, while the on-line type gives a clean, stable sine-wave output.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p321","order":3495},
{"id":"p3-b10-031","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"At what load range do most UPS systems operate, and at what load range should their efficiency be demonstrated?","back":"Most operate at 25% to 60% of nominal load. Efficiency should be shown at 25% to 50% load.","explain":"Most UPSs peak in efficiency between 50% and 100% load, so a full-load figure overstates real performance. Most computer and server loads are non-linear, which also lowers UPS efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p322","order":3496},
{"id":"p3-b10-032","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"problem","front":"A UPS draws 100 kW from the utility and delivers 90 kW to the critical loads, with the difference lost as heat in conversion. What is its efficiency?","back":"90%.","explain":"UPS efficiency is the useful energy out divided by the energy in. The conversion loss is the difference between the two, and it is released as heat.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p322","steps":[{"prompt":"Identify the input and the useful output.","answer":"Input 100 kW; useful output 90 kW.","explain":"Efficiency compares energy that reaches the loads with energy drawn from the utility."},{"prompt":"Find the conversion loss.","answer":"100 kW − 90 kW = 10 kW lost as heat.","explain":"The loss happens in the transformers, rectifiers and inverters inside the UPS."},{"prompt":"Compute the efficiency.","answer":"90 ÷ 100 = 0.90, or 90%.","explain":"Efficiency is output divided by input."},{"prompt":"Check the loading point.","answer":"A figure at full load may overstate performance, since most UPS run at 25% to 60% load. Test at 25% to 50% load.","explain":"The chapter asks for efficiency at the loads where UPSs usually operate."}],"order":3497},
{"id":"p3-b10-033","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What power is measured to assess the energy performance of a lift, and what is measured for an escalator?","back":"Lift: power at rated load moving upward at contract speed. Escalator or passenger conveyor: power with no load, running at rated speed (up or down).","explain":"An escalator’s rated load is given as a number of persons, not in kg, so there is no theoretical rated load to test with. The no-load running power shows the fixed friction overhead.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p322","order":3498},
{"id":"p3-b10-034","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What are the four categories of energy consumed by lifts and escalators?","back":"Friction losses while travelling; dynamic losses during starting and stopping; lifting (or lowering) work, i.e. potential energy transfer; and regeneration into the supply system.","explain":"The general approach to efficiency is to minimise friction and dynamic losses. Specifying efficient equipment, avoiding over-design and zoning the lift system are the main principles.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p323","order":3499},
{"id":"p3-b10-035","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What is the function of a building management system (BMS or BEMS)?","back":"Monitoring and control of a building’s services and functions in a way that is economical and efficient in energy use. One system can also control a group of buildings.","explain":"BEMS ranges from a simple timer for on and off schedules to a computerised central controller fed by sensors. The chapter notes that good results need proper design, installation and commissioning.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p324","order":3500},
{"id":"p3-b10-036","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"How does the national star rating for office buildings work, and what does 5 star mean?","back":"Buildings are rated on a 1 to 5 star scale based on specific energy use in kWh/m²/yr. 5 star is the most efficient. A label is valid for 5 years from the date of issue.","explain":"The rating normalises for operating hours, climate zone and conditioned space, and compares a building with its peer group. It acts as an external benchmark that helps owners find buildings with the best opportunities for improvement.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p324","order":3501},
{"id":"p3-b10-037","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"In the New Delhi (composite zone) example, what EPI gives a 5-star rating, and what is the 1-star band?","back":"An EPI below 90 kWh/m²/yr gives 5 stars, for a building with more than 50% air-conditioned area. The 1-star band is 165 to 190 kWh/m²/yr.","explain":"The EPI bandwidth depends on the climate zone and the air-conditioned share of the built-up area. New Delhi is the chapter’s worked example for the composite zone.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p325","order":3502},
{"id":"p3-b10-038","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"problem","front":"A BPO building has an EPI of 150 kWh/m²/yr. It operates 8 hours a day, 5 days a week. What is its AAhEPI in (Wh/m²)/hr?","back":"About 72.1 (Wh/m²)/hr.","explain":"AAhEPI converts an annual energy index into an hourly index, so BPO buildings with different operating hours can be compared on one scale. The formula uses 52 weeks to convert the weekly schedule to a year.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p325","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the AAhEPI formula.","answer":"AAhEPI = [EPI ÷ (daily hours × days per week × 52)] × 1000","explain":"The 52 converts the weekly schedule into annual operating hours."},{"prompt":"Find the annual operating hours.","answer":"8 × 5 × 52 = 2080 h/yr","explain":"Multiply daily hours, days per week and weeks per year."},{"prompt":"Divide the EPI by the operating hours.","answer":"150 ÷ 2080 = 0.0721 kWh/m²/hr","explain":"This gives the average hourly energy per square metre."},{"prompt":"Convert to Wh and round.","answer":"0.0721 × 1000 ≈ 72.1 (Wh/m²)/hr","explain":"The ×1000 converts kWh to Wh, which is the AAhEPI unit."}],"order":3503},
{"id":"p3-b10-039","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What unit is AAhEPI given in for BPO buildings, and what is excluded from its electricity total?","back":"(Wh/m²)/hr. Electricity generated from on-site renewable sources, such as solar PV, is excluded from the total.","explain":"AAhEPI uses purchased and generated electricity divided by built-up area and total annual operating hours. The result does not depend on time of day, so it applies to all BPO operations.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p325","order":3504},
{"id":"p3-b10-040","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What temperature and relative humidity does the chapter recommend for comfort in air-conditioned spaces?","back":"Temperature between 23 °C and 25 °C, and relative humidity between 55% and 65%.","explain":"The chapter gives these as the comfort setpoints for air-conditioned spaces. Holding them, rather than cooling further, is the basis of the AC measures that follow in the same section.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p326","order":3505},
{"id":"p3-b10-041","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"What saving do compact fluorescent lamps (CFLs) give over incandescent bulbs?","back":"About 75% less electricity, with no reduction in illumination levels.","explain":"A lamp’s lighting efficacy (lumens per watt) decides how many watts a given light output needs. CFLs are the chapter’s example of a high-efficiency replacement for incandescent lamps.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p327","order":3506},
{"id":"p3-b10-042","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"fact","front":"Above what floor area must interior lighting have an automatic control device, and when must occupancy sensors switch lights off?","back":"Buildings larger than 500 m² (5,000 ft²). Occupancy sensors must turn lights off within 30 minutes of an occupant leaving the space.","explain":"Office areas below 30 m², meeting and conference rooms, school classrooms and storage spaces must have occupancy sensors. Other spaces can use scheduled control, with independent schedules for areas up to 2,500 m² on one floor.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p319","order":3507},
{"id":"p3-b10-043","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"problem","front":"A plant has 200 fluorescent fittings. Conventional ballasts lose 12 W each and electronic ballasts lose 2 W each. How much power is saved by replacing all of them?","back":"2,000 W, or 2 kW.","explain":"Ballast losses add to the lamp power of every fitting, so each electronic ballast saves 10 W. Across many fittings this adds up quickly, and the saving is a continuous reduction in electricity draw.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p327","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the saving per fitting.","answer":"12 W − 2 W = 10 W","explain":"Compare the loss of the conventional ballast with that of the electronic one."},{"prompt":"Multiply by the number of fittings.","answer":"200 × 10 W = 2000 W","explain":"Each fitting makes the same saving."},{"prompt":"Express the saving in kilowatts.","answer":"2000 W = 2 kW","explain":"This is the reduction in connected load."},{"prompt":"Convert to annual energy (illustrative).","answer":"At 6000 h/yr, 2 kW × 6000 h = 12,000 kWh/yr","explain":"The 6000 h/yr operating time is an assumed example, not a value from the chapter."}],"order":3508},
{"id":"p3-b10-044","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"problem","front":"A compressor draws 100 kW at 8 kg/cm² discharge pressure. Reducing discharge pressure to 7 kg/cm² saves how much power, by the chapter rule of thumb?","back":"About 9%, which is about 9 kW. Compressed-air leakage also falls by about 10%.","explain":"Each 1 kg/cm² of pressure reduction cuts input power, and it also cuts leakage, since leak rates rise with pressure. The chapter advises setting discharge pressure to the lowest acceptable value.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p333","steps":[{"prompt":"Identify the chapter rule.","answer":"Reducing air pressure by 1 kg/cm² (8 to 7) saves 9% of input power.","explain":"This is the chapter’s compressed-air rule of thumb."},{"prompt":"Calculate the power saving.","answer":"9% × 100 kW = 9 kW","explain":"Apply the percentage to the compressor’s input power."},{"prompt":"Note the leakage effect.","answer":"Compressed-air leakage falls by about 10%.","explain":"Lower pressure means less air escapes through leaks, so less air must be compressed."}],"order":3509},
{"id":"p3-b10-045","paper":3,"topic":"Buildings & ECBC","kind":"problem","front":"A chiller compressor draws 200 kW. Lowering the condensing temperature by 5.5 °C cuts compressor power by 20% to 25%. What is the saving, and what is the new compressor power?","back":"A saving of 40 to 50 kW, so the compressor draws about 150 to 160 kW.","explain":"Cooler condenser water lowers the pressure the compressor must work against, so less work is needed. The chapter also advises using the lowest-temperature condenser water the chiller can handle.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 10 p334","steps":[{"prompt":"Identify the chapter rule.","answer":"Reducing condensing temperature by 5.5 °C reduces compressor power by 20% to 25%.","explain":"This is a rule of thumb from the chapter’s chiller checklist."},{"prompt":"Calculate the lower-bound saving.","answer":"20% × 200 kW = 40 kW","explain":"Use the low end of the range for a conservative figure."},{"prompt":"Calculate the upper-bound saving.","answer":"25% × 200 kW = 50 kW","explain":"Use the high end of the range for an optimistic figure."},{"prompt":"Find the new compressor power.","answer":"200 − 50 = 150 kW to 200 − 40 = 160 kW","explain":"Subtract each saving from the original 200 kW draw."}],"order":3510}
]$cards$::jsonb);
